 "asked_at": "2026-10-18T00:45+08:00",
 "answer": "They may be showing **different measures or time windows**, so the labels don’t necessarily conflict.\n\n- In the U.S., apps often show the **AQI**. Its category depends on the pollutant driving the reading; “Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups” starts at AQI 101, while “Unhealthy” starts at 151. ([epa.gov](https://www.epa.gov/outdoor-air-quality-data/airdata-basic-information?utm_source=openai))\n- If by **PSI** you mean Singapore’s Pollutant Standards Index, it reflects roughly the **past 24 hours**. A separate PM2.5 reading can reflect more recent conditions, so it may look worse than the PSI. ([nea.gov.sg](https://www.nea.gov.sg/media/readers-letters/index/pm-2-5-for-immediate-activities-psi-forecast-for-next-day?utm_source=openai))\n- Apps can also use different nearby monitors, update times, or forecasts—and “unhealthy” may be a general warning rather than the official category.\n\nCheck the **number, pollutant, timestamp, and exact label** in each app. If you share your location and the two readings (or app names), I can help compare them.",
